  "reason": "The records collectively supply several relevant ingredients: rapid cooling can kinetically retain metastable phases (R13); processing, composition, and cooling can control multiphase HEA structures (R1, R10, R14-R17); Laves phases can form through chemical partitioning during solidification (R1, R17); as-cast eutectic HEAs can exhibit compositionally segregated dual phases (R7, R9, R20); and antiferromagnetic constituents can reduce saturation magnetization in otherwise ferromagnetic HEAs (R8). They also show generic strengthening or pinning effects from hard secondary phases and interfaces (R12, R16). However, none identifies FeCoNiTi, the fcc+C14 combination, the matched C14-dominant comparator, Ti-sublattice antiferromagnetic coupling, phase-specific transitions, or enhancement of coercivity through orbital/spin-canted domain-wall pinning. Thus the literature points broadly toward rapid-solidification-controlled dual-phase magnetic HEAs, but reconstructing the specific intervention and magnetic mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
